## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design an end-to-end event-driven pipeline with Rekognition Video, including asynchronous results and integration with DynamoDB and S3. It also exercises privacy, error handling, and cost trade-offs in a beginner-friendly AWS setup.

## Key Concepts
- Rekognition Video asynchronous APIs (moderation, labels)
- SNS/SQS notifications and S3 event triggers
- Lambda orchestration and retries
- DynamoDB upsert patterns and compact metadata design
- IAM least privilege and KMS for encryption
- Privacy, retention, and cost considerations

## Code Example
```javascript
// process Rekognition output and update DynamoDB
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const ddb = new AWS.DynamoDB.DocumentClient();
async function upsertVideoIndex(videoId, s3Key, labels, status){
  const item = { videoId, s3Key, labelList: labels, moderationStatus: status, timestamp: Date.now() };
  await ddb.put({TableName:'VideoIndex', Item:item}).promise();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale this to 1000+ concurrent videos and handle partial failures?
- What changes would you make to support different video formats and longer durations while controlling costs?

Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design a scalable, privacy-aware, vendor-integrated NLP pipeline that processes structured chat data in JSONL format, with PII redaction, cross-account sharing, and cost-aware inference choices.

## Key Concepts

- Event-driven architectures (S3 events, Step Functions)
- Lambda for lightweight processing and redaction
- Amazon Comprehend for NER/Sentiment
- SageMaker for topic modeling and batch inference
- Data governance: IAM, KMS, encryption, access control
- Observability: CloudWatch, X-Ray, metrics, traces
- Cross-account sharing patterns and vendor approvals

## Code Example

```javascript
// Lambda: simple PII redaction sketch
exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const body = JSON.parse(event.body);
  body.messages = body.messages.map(m => m.replace(/[A-Z0-9._%+-]+@[A-Z0-9.-]+\.[A-Z]{2,4}/gi, '[REDACTED_EMAIL]'));
  return { statusCode: 200, body: JSON.stringify(body) };
};
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure latency and cold-start impact for this pipeline at scale?
- How would you implement data retention policies and automated revocation of access for vendors?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of event-driven AWS architectures, video processing workflows, and end-to-end data indexing, with emphasis on security, reliability, and cost optimization.

## Key Concepts

- Event-driven orchestration using S3 event notifications and Step Functions
- AWS Elemental MediaConvert for multi-format video processing (MP4, GIF thumbnails)
- DynamoDB indexing with comprehensive video metadata
- Security best practices: IAM least privilege, encryption at rest and in transit, access logging
- Robust error handling: retry mechanisms, dead-letter queues, comprehensive monitoring
- Cost optimization strategies: appropriate resource sizing, storage lifecycle policies, and efficient processing patterns

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example Lambda snippet: update DynamoDB after MediaConvert completion
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const ddb = new AWS.DynamoDB.DocumentClient();

exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const { jobId, status, output } = event.detail;

  if (status === 'COMPLETE') {
    const params = {
      TableName: process.env.VIDEOS_TABLE,
      Item: {
        videoId: output.videoId,
        sourceKey: output.inputFile,
        thumbnailKey: output.thumbnailPath,
        previewKey: output.mp4Path,
        duration: output.duration,
        encodingQuality: '1080p',
        processedAt: new Date().toISOString()
      }
    };

    await ddb.put(params).promise();
  }
};
```
